{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.82,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": [
        "C12"
      ],
      "pins": [
        "C12.1",
        "C12.2"
      ],
      "nets": [
        "+3V3",
        "GND"
      ],
      "observed": "C12 is listed as a 1 mF capacitor in an 0805 footprint and is connected between the 3.3 V rail and GND, while its LCSC field is C28323, the same LCSC part used for the 1 uF decoupling capacitors.",
      "expected": "As a decoupling/bypass capacitor on the 3.3 V logic rail in an 0805 package, C12 should be in the nF-to-uF range consistent with its role and LCSC part. A literal 1 mF value is inconsistent with the footprint and the stated decoupling use.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"C12\") (value \"1mF\") (footprint \"Capacitor_SMD:C_0805_2012Metric\")"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(field (name \"LCSC\") \"C28323\")"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"1\") (name \"+3V3\") ... (node (ref \"C12\") (pin \"1\")"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (code \"13\") (name \"GND\") ... (node (ref \"C12\") (pin \"2\")"
        },
        {
          "source": "spice",
          "quote": "C12 +3V3 0 1m"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "decoupling caps `C3/C4/C6/C8/C9/C10/C12`"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If assembled as a 1000 uF capacitor, the 3.3 V rail would have excessive bulk capacitance and could cause slow startup or regulator inrush/current-limiting problems. If the LCSC part is actually 1 uF, the schematic/BOM value is wrong and could cause a sourcing or assembly mistake.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Most connectivity matches the specification, but C12 is specified as a 1 mF capacitor on the 3.3 V rail in an 0805 footprint, which is inconsistent with its decoupling role and LCSC part and indicates a wrong-value anomaly."
}

